3 — प्रोटोकॉल-कंसेप्टेड अल्फाबेट (गहरे भाग)

> नोट: नीचे जितने भी "attack vectors" बताए गए हैं — वे सब कॉन्सेप्चुअल और डिटेक्शन-फोकस्ड हैं। किसी भी production पर तुरंत लागू करने से पहले RoE + legal चाहिए।




---

A. ARP (Address Resolution Protocol)

क्या है: LAN में IP ↔ MAC mapping।
खतरा (conceptual): ARP spoofing/poisoning → MITM, traffic interception, man-in-the-switch.
Rare/Advanced: Gratuitous-ARP storms to cause CAM churn and micro-DOS; ARP cache timing side-channels.
Indicators: duplicate ARP replies, frequent ARP table changes, hosts suddenly seeing multiple MACs, sudden flows re-routing.
Defense/Detect: Dynamic ARP Inspection, static ARP on hosts for critical servers, Zeek/IDS alerts on ARP anomalies, SIEM rule: ARP reply rate > baseline.
Log/ Evidence: ARP logs (Zeek/pcap), switch MAC table snapshots (timestamped).


---

B. DHCP

क्या है: IP lease assignment.
खतरा: Rogue DHCP server → wrong gateway/DNS → silent MITM or DoS. DHCP starvation used to cause outage.
Rare/Advanced: DHCP option manipulation delivering malicious PXE boot or malicious DNS server (supply chain for boot).
Indicators: multiple DHCPOFFERs, unexpected gateway/DNS in leases, frequent DISCOVER storms.
Defense: DHCP snooping, rate limits, bind IP/port leases with IP Source Guard, inventory of authorized DHCP servers.
Logs: DHCP server leases, switch DHCP snooping bindings, pcap of DHCP handshake.


---

C. DNS (incl. DNSSEC)

क्या है: नाम समाधान; तीसरी आँख।
खतरा: cache poisoning, spoofing, DNS tunnelling (data-exfil), DNS rebinding.
Rare/Advanced: Fast flux + DNS over unusual ports; covert exfil via long TXT records or high-entropy subdomains.
Indicators: sudden spike in NXDOMAIN, long/large TXT responses, bursts of subdomain queries with long labels, abnormal query entropy.
Defense/Detect: DNSSEC for integrity, restrict recursive resolvers, Response Policy Zones (RPZ), DNS logging + anomaly detection (Zeek dns.log), detect entropy & length anomalies. GIAC papers and practical research describe detection heuristics for DNS tunnelling. 
Logs: full DNS logs, Zeek dns.log, recursive resolver logs, PCAP of suspicious DNS flows.


---

D. HTTP / HTTPS / HTTP/2 / HTTP Request Smuggling

क्या है: Web traffic; app-layer.
खतरा (conceptual): clear-text HTTP → creds/session leak; TLS misconfig → downgrade; HTTP request smuggling/desync (esp. with proxies and HTTP/2 complexities).
Rare/Advanced: HTTP/2 specific implementations issues (pipeline & header compression differences) led to novel desyncs—PortsWigger research highlighted new attack classes. 
Indicators: odd request/response alignment between proxy & origin, unexplained 5xx/empty responses, mismatch in Content-Length vs Transfer-Encoding handling logs.
Defense/Detect: enforce single canonical parsing chain (disable ambiguous proxies), use WAF that understands HTTP/2/1.1 differences, log raw request/response timings, keep TLS strong (NIST TLS guidelines). 
Logs: web server access logs, proxy logs, WAF alerts, Zeek http.log; TLS metadata (JA3/JA3S fingerprints — see below). 


---

E. TLS/SSL (certificate, handshake, fingerprints)

क्या है: encryption & auth for apps.
खतरा (conceptual): weak ciphers/downgrade, misissued certs, improper hostname validation, PKI abuse (CA compromise).
Rare/Advanced: JA3/JA3S fingerprinting reveals client/server TLS stacks — attackers can mimic benign JA3s to blend in; defenders use JA3/JA3S + CT logs to detect anomalies. Zeek tooling and JA3 fingerprints are industry staples. 
Indicators: suspicious JA3s on unusual endpoints, certificate transparency anomalies, expired/weak certs, TLS version downgrades.
Defense: enforce TLS 1.2+/1.3, strong ciphers, HSTS, certificate pinning for critical apps, monitor CT logs and JA3 anomalies.
Logs: tls.log (Zeek), JA3 hashes, certificate details, CT monitor outputs.


---

F. QUIC (and HTTP/3)

क्या है: UDP-based transport with built-in crypto; different semantics than TCP.
खतरा: new protocol surface → implementation inconsistencies, path migration, and state machine differences cause novel bugs. Recent surveys found multiple implementation security considerations. 
Indicators: unexpected QUIC handshakes, migration events, unusual packet patterns on UDP ports 443.
Defense: patch/monitor implementations, use IDS that understands QUIC metadata, collect JA3-style QUIC fingerprints where possible.
Logs: QUIC handshake metadata, UDP flow logs, server QUIC logs.


---

G. TCP-level issues (fragmentation, RST injection, SYN floods, ACK storms)

क्या है: transport layer semantics.
खतरा: fragmentation evasion (overlap/teardrop style), forged RSTs to break sessions, SYN floods (DoS).
Rare/Advanced: crafted overlapping fragments to evade IDS signature engines (fragmentation overlap evasion). Modern IDS/OS have mitigations, but misconfigured stacks remain vulnerable.
Indicators: high fragment counts, inconsistent reassembly seen in IDS vs end-host, many RSTs from non-expected sources.
Defense/Detect: TCP stack hardening, fragmentation reassembly policy alignment between IDS and endpoints, SYN cookies, rate limiting.
Logs: NetFlow + PCAP, Suricata/TCP anomaly alerts, host TCP stack logs.


---

H. ICMP / NTP / Time protocols

क्या है: diagnostics & time sync.
खतरा: NTP spoofing causing time shifts (impacts cert validation / logs), ICMP used as covert channel or recon.
Rare/Advanced: time-shift to break logging correlation and replay attacks.
Indicators: large clock offsets, unusual NTP servers in logs, ICMP payloads with high entropy.
Defense: use authenticated NTP, restrict NTP sources, ICMP rate limits and inspect payload patterns.
Logs: NTP logs, ICMP flow logs, Zeek icmp.log.


---

I. IPv6 (NDP, Router Advertisements, SLAAC)

क्या है: IPv6 neighbor discovery & addressing.
खतरा: Rogue RA → default gateway takeover, NDP spoofing → MITM. RA-Guard and implementation guidance exist. RFCs discuss RA-Guard approaches. 
Indicators: sudden attacker RAs, new IPv6 addresses on hosts, NDP anomalies.
Defense: RA-Guard, RA filtering on edge, SEND where feasible, firewall rules for IPv6.
Logs: ICMPv6 logs, NDP caches, switch port NDP captures.


---

J. SMB / NTLM / Kerberos (Windows ecosystem)

क्या है: file sharing & auth.
खतरा: SMBv1 vulnerabilities, NTLM relay, weak Kerberos configs → lateral movement, RCE.
Rare/Advanced: NTLM relay to NTLM-authenticated services that accept relay, unconstrained delegation abuse.
Indicators: SMBv1 protocol presence, many failed NTLM attempts, unusual TGS/TGT behavior in logs.
Defense: disable SMBv1, enforce SMB signing, move to Kerberos with constrained delegation, EDR detection of lateral movement.
Logs: SMB logs, Windows security logs (Kerberos/NTLM), Zeek smb.log.


---

K. SMTP / Email protocols

क्या है: mail transfer & authentication.
खतरा: open relays, spoofing, BEC, control of MX/DKIM/SPF causing phishing success.
Rare/Advanced: DKIM private key leakage, MTA misconfig allowing header injection.
Indicators: outbound spam spikes, forged headers, DKIM/SPF failures.
Defense: SPF/DKIM/DMARC, outbound mail filtering, MTA hardening, mailbox MFA & DMARC enforcement.
Logs: MTA logs, spam gateway logs, DMARC reports.


---

L. SNMP / Management protocols

क्या है: device management.
खतरा: SNMPv1/2 unprotected strings → config disclosure; misconfigured management endpoints (Telnet/old SSH) exposed.
Rare/Advanced: SNMP write privileges abused to reconfigure router ACLs.
Indicators: SNMP GET/SET from unexpected hosts, plain-text community strings in PCAP.
Defense: SNMPv3, restrict collectors, management plane isolation.
Logs: SNMP logs, device config change history.


---

M. BGP / Routing Protocols

क्या है: Internet routing; huge blast radius.
खतरा: prefix hijack, route leaks — can re-route or blackhole traffic. MANRS / RPKI mitigate this. 
Rare/Advanced: transient path hijacks that drop traffic for milliseconds to exfiltrate sessions; BGP route manipulation used for mass interception.
Indicators: AS path changes, sudden traceroute path divergence, BGP updates with new origin AS.
Defense: RPKI, prefix filtering, BGP monitoring/alerting, route coincidence detection.
Logs: BGP update streams, route collector feeds, traceroute baselines.


