4 — “जो लोग नहीं जानते” — Hidden / Rare, High-Impact Nuggets (must-know)

1. HTTP/2 & HTTP/1.1 desync revival — modern research showed new HTTP/2 exclusive smuggling classes; proxies and servers parse differently → headroom for stealthy request splitting. 


2. QUIC implementation differences — QUIC moves semantics to userland; multiple implementations have subtle differences that can be attacked or fingerprinted. 


3. JA3/JA3S fingerprint evasion — attackers can craft TLS clients that mimic benign fingerprints; defenders should combine JA3 with behavioral context. 


4. DNS tunnelling at scale is noisy but stealthy — detection based on entropy + unusual TTL/label lengths works well (GIAC research). 


5. BGP transient hijacks as interception windows — very short route hijacks can still capture sessions; RPKI + monitoring helps. 


6. IPv6 often unmonitored — attack surface where RA / NDP are ignored; RA-Guard + RFC implementation guidance needed. 




